AB  - Sustainable development encompasses the integration of social, environmental, and economic dimensions, making it a complex and multifaceted issue, with no single, definitive solution for its achievement (Hedenus et al., 2018). This complexity places new demands on education, especially in preparing young learners to navigate and engage with uncertain and often conflicting sustainability challenges. In Swedish primary education, teachers are tasked with incorporating education for sustainable development (ESD) into their teaching. Research underscores their pivotal role in preparing future generations to address sustainability challenges (Ideland, 2016) and in educating children about sustainable practices (Hedefalk et al., 2014). The aim of ESD is not merely to deliver predetermined content but to foster a sense of agency by encouraging pupils’ active engagement with sustainability issues (Häggström, 2022). This pedagogical emphasis on pupil participation is also underlined by Lundegård and Caiman (2019), who argue for the importance of pupil influence in ESD. Despite this emphasis, empirical research on how ESD is practically enacted in primary school classrooms remains limited, particularly in relation to pupils’ influence. Reflection can serve as a way forward for teachers to examine their pedagogical approaches (Schön, 1983; Thompson &amp; Thompson, 2023) and navigate the complexities of integrating sustainability into education. It could o er an opportunity for teachers to develop their practices by linking reflection with action (Rolfe, 2011), thereby enabling them to respond to the evolving challenges and intricacies of ESD. Moreover, collaboration among teachers may further enrich this reflective process by fostering the exchange of insights, challenging assumptions, and facilitating mutual learning from others’ experiences. This collaborative reflection can provide opportunities to explore innovative strategies and incorporate diverse perspectives into pedagogical practice. As Korthagen and Nuijten (2022) argue, engaging with colleagues’ perspectives in reflective practices can enhance professional growth. Given this context, the aim of this study is to investigate how teachers conceptualize their teaching practices to create opportunities for pupils’ influence in ESD. This study is grounded in sociocultural theory, which emphasizes the role of social interactions and collaborative practices in learning, mediated by language and artefacts (Katsell &amp; Wertsch, 2002; Vygotskij, 1986; Vygotsky, 1978). The empirical material consists of teachers’ reflections, conducted in pairs before and after lessons. Preliminary tendencies show that the teachers’ reflections reveal nuanced views on pupils’ influence in ESD, highlighting both opportunities and challenges in facilitating ESD in classrooms. These findings underscore the importance of continuous reflection in developing teaching practices that acknowledge both the complexities of sustainability and the role of pupils in shaping their own learning experiences in ESD. 
