AB  - Many children in need of social support often go unnoticed and miss out on the help they require. Support needs can be identified by adults around the child or by the children themselves, but the latter requires the support to be visible and accessible. This article, based on a previous case study, shows that professionals conduct outreach to identify children who do not seek support independently. Their approach needs to be adaptable, considering the child’s specific situation. Professionals rely on their judgment and practical experience to tailor their efforts, but this requires time, resources, and flexibility. The professionals emphasize the importance of building trustful relationships with children, making them feel comfortable seeking support when needed. However, this preventive and promotive work can be hindered if too many resources are spent on remedial tasks. The professionals also stress the value of commitment and using personal connection in relationship-building. For better identification of children in need, the professionals need a broader understanding of the children’s needs. © w&amp;b. 
